sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Size of curvature and time errors when the assumptions fail" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> ;
    dct:description "Quantify the deviations the problem asks about, as a model bound rather than a plant measurement. Start from the two-layer Euler-Bernoulli equilibrium already proved in Lean and check that a numerical beam recovers κ = (3/2) Δε / h when thicknesses and moduli are equal, to within 1 percent. Then solve three extensions the theorems do not cover: a linear through-thickness modulus gradient, a standard-linear-solid viscoelastic branch in the active layer, and a fibre angle that adds the shear mismatch already defined for the antisymmetric-twist claim. On a grid of modulus ratio from 0.1 to 10, gradient strength, viscosity ratio and fibre angle from 0 to 45 degrees, record κ divided by the Timoshenko value and the apparent exponent d log τ / d log h." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The grid of κ / κ_Timoshenko and of the apparent time exponent. Deviations are large, by the pre-registered rule, where the absolute relative curvature error exceeds 0.50 or the time exponent leaves [1.5, 2.5]. The symmetric-case check must pass or the grid is void." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The solver misses the Lean symmetric curvature by more than 1 percent, or the error changes sign when the mesh is refined by two, so the deviation table is a discretisation artefact." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Verify the numerical beam against bilayerCurvature_symmetric to 1 percent. 2. Add the modulus gradient, the viscoelastic branch and the fibre angle one at a time. 3. Sweep the pre-registered grid. 4. Tabulate relative curvature error and the apparent time exponent. 5. Mark cells where the absolute relative curvature error exceeds 0.50 or the time exponent leaves [1.5, 2.5]." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-01> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0184> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do real hygromorphs follow bilayer curvature and diffusive response-time scaling?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0184> dct:title "Hygromorphs: from pine cones to biomimetic bilayers" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Reyssat and Mahadevan 2009, Hygromorphs: from pine cones to biomimetic bilayers" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> dct:title "Analysis of Bi-Metal Thermostats" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Timoshenko 1925, Analysis of bi-metal thermostats" .
}

